/** @page contributing How the code is written

  @section gated What the gate enforces

  Formatting and lint are not advice here: `./scripts/ci.sh` runs `clang-format --dry-run --Werror` over every source file and
  `clang-tidy` over every translation unit with the rules in `.clang-tidy`, and a finding fails the check. There is no separate
  "style backlog" to consult - the number is zero, and the gate keeps it there.

  The rules that shape code most are: braces on every statement body; `nodiscard` on an accessor whose result a caller must use;
  designated initialisers for aggregates; `contains` rather than `find() != end()`; constants named `kPascalCase`; and the
  naming table in [conventions](https://github.com/BHare1985/mosaic/blob/main/docs/design/conventions.md).

  @section review The two rules review checks

  - **No `std::` in a public header.** A header may not drag a `std::` name into `mosaic` for every includer.
  - **No `using namespace` anywhere.** A `.cpp` may import the specific names it uses; nothing imports a namespace.

  Both are stated in [conventions](https://github.com/BHare1985/mosaic/blob/main/docs/design/conventions.md), and both are the
  kind of thing a compiler will not tell you about until it is a habit.

  @section adding Adding a rule

  In this order, always: enable the check, fix every finding, then gate it. Adopting a rule by turning the gate on first leaves
  the tree red for everyone and teaches people to ignore the check; fixing first and gating later leaves nobody to keep it.
*/
